@CarnaViire, thanks for the questions. My WS impl only acts as the server for my connections, so for my service, this only happens for the server side of the WS connection. I'm unsure if the error only occurs under load due to the nature of the crash. It happens on more used servers, but that could be because the code is used more. It is hard to say if the crash is more likely when the server is more in use, which follows a daily cycle pattern.

I was trying to make an isolate repo, but I couldn't get it happening. Since my service handles ~100 websocket connects/disconnected an hour with thousands of messages, it's not super likely to crash, but it does crash around 2-3 times a day.

Sorry, that's not very helpful; let me know if there's anything else I can get you that would be more helpful.

If you don't have any sensitive data, would you mind getting a memory dump when the segfault occurs? If you can't share it, I can try to guide you for what exactly we're looking for so you can inspect the memory dump yourself.

Also, would it be possible for you to try and disable context takeover to see if the error would stop occurring? To do this, you should do the following:

```c#
var ws = await m_context.WebSockets.AcceptWebSocketAsync(new WebSocketAcceptContext() 
{ 
    DangerousEnableCompression = true,
    DisableServerContextTakeover = true
});
```

